# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Firebase Auth configuration (all values are read from the runtime env and
# inlined into both the server and the browser bundle by Next.js).
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (git-ignored) for local development, and set the same
# variables in the hosting provider (Vercel → Project → Environment Variables).
#
# Every NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_* value below is optional at build time: when a
# variable is missing the app falls back to the documented fallback project and
# says so in the console + in the in-app diagnostics panel. In production, set
# them all (an incomplete set is the most common cause of
# `auth/invalid-api-key`, `auth/configuration-not-found` and redirect failures).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "firebase" → real Firebase Auth · "demo" → on-device gateway · "auto" → Firebase
# when the config below is complete, demo otherwise. Default when unset: demo.
NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_BACKEND=firebase

NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=AIzaSyAbb9aCsOfMkw9G5H0L58LSkbsONhix51k
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=agriculture-intelligente-7873e.firebaseapp.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=agriculture-intelligente-7873e
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=agriculture-intelligente-7873e.firebasestorage.app
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=213483282746
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:213483282746:web:badde539687095497a7003
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-VKD4SWJLNJ

# Firebase Console → Authentication → Settings → Authorized domains must list
# every origin the app is served from, including the Arena preview host
# (*.e2b.app) and http://localhost:3000 for local development.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AI assistant — the plant-diagnosis orchestrator behind /api/assistant
# (server-only secrets; NO NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix so they are never inlined into
# the browser bundle).
#
# An attached photo runs a strict priority chain; a text-only question skips
# the image stage and goes straight to the text stages.
#
# GEMINI_API_KEY       → Google AI Studio → Get API key (aistudio.google.com/apikey)
#                        Used in TWO roles:
#                          • Step 1 PRIMARY IMAGE MODEL — Gemini inspects the
#                            photo and is pinned by responseMimeType
#                            "application/json" + a responseSchema to answer
#                            with a structured AnalysisData object
#                            { plant_type, disease_detected, disease_name,
#                              confidence, affected_parts, severity,
#                              symptoms_observed, notes }. It receives the
#                            ORIGINAL frame, not the Step 0 crop, because it
#                            reasons over the whole scene. Default id
#                            gemini-3.8-flash (→ gemini-3.5-flash →
#                            gemini-3.5-flash-lite when an id is retired; pin
#                            another id with GEMINI_MODEL below) via
#                            https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/<model>:generateContent
#                            under ONE shared 18 s AbortController deadline for
#                            the whole model chain.
#                          • Step 3 TEXT FALLBACK — reached ONLY after the
#                            Hugging Face text model below failed. It gets the
#                            AnalysisData and NO image, so it formats the data
#                            and never re-analyses the photo.
#                        Step 1 counts Gemini as FAILED — and only then falls
#                        back to MobileNetV2 — on: (a) an API/network error or
#                        timeout; (b) Gemini refusing or returning no usable
#                        text; (c) a response that is not valid JSON / not an
#                        object / missing a required field. A LOW "confidence"
#                        is explicitly NOT a failure: the orchestrator has no
#                        confidence threshold, so an unsure but well-formed
#                        verdict is passed straight through to the text stage
#                        and MobileNetV2 is never consulted.
#                        When BOTH image models fail, no text model is called
#                        with empty data: the route answers 200 with a polite,
#                        pre-written "retry with a clearer photo" message
#                        ({ source: "direct", analysisSource: null,
#                        textSource: null }).
#                        Multi-key rotation: EVERY variable starting with
#                        GEMINI_API_KEY participates — GEMINI_API_KEY, the
#                        GEMINI_API_KEYS comma-separated pool, and numbered
#                        variants (GEMINI_API_KEY_1, GEMINI_API_KEY_2, …).
#                        They are combined, trimmed and deduplicated into one
#                        pool. On rate-limit errors (HTTP 429,
#                        RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED, quota limit) the route rotates to
#                        the next key and retries until one answers or all
#                        keys are exhausted, after which the built-in
#                        formatter answers locally. A successful answer
#                        returns HTTP 200 { source: "llm" } (source: "hybrid"
#                        when it ships with a diagnosis).
#
# GEMINI_MODEL (OPTIONAL, server-only) — the Gemini model id, used for BOTH
#                        Gemini roles (Step 1 analysis and Step 3 text
#                        fallback). Default: gemini-3.8-flash. Pin any other
#                        generateContent-capable id. A retired/mistyped id
#                        still walks the built-in fallback chain
#                        (gemini-3.5-flash → gemini-3.5-flash-lite), so an
#                        override can never brick the stage. Blank or unset =
#                        the gemini-3.8-flash default.
#
#                        The chain is defined in ONE place,
#                        src/lib/assistant/gemini-models.ts, and deliberately
#                        mixes a current model with LONG-LIVED ones: Google
#                        rotates the 3.6/3.7/3.8 Flash ids, while
#                        gemini-3.5-flash and gemini-3.5-flash-lite are
#                        supported into 2027. A chain of short-availability
#                        ids alone is how a single deprecation took every
#                        production request down at once.
#
#                        Verify the ids against Google's live catalog:
#                          npm run check:models      # human-readable
#                          npm run check:models -- --json   # for CI
#                        Exit code 0 = every configured id is live; 1 = an id
#                        is missing or ListModels could not be reached. The
#                        same check runs automatically inside the server
#                        (once per process, 6 h TTL) and logs a "[Gemini
#                        Health]" verdict — a dead id is then dropped from
#                        the request chain instead of 404-ing on every photo.
#
# HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY  → Hugging Face → Settings → Access Tokens. Create a
#                        fine-grained token and tick "Make calls to Inference
#                        Providers" (a plain read token 401/403s on the router).
#                        Used in TWO roles:
#                          • Step 1 FALLBACK IMAGE MODEL — the PlantVillage
#                            leaf-disease classifier on the hf-inference
#                            provider:
#                            linkanjarad/mobilenet_v2_1.0_224-plant-disease-
#                            identification (MobileNetV2 — the single clean
#                            default; the 400-prone cascade ids are removed).
#                            Reached ONLY when the Gemini image analysis
#                            failed. It receives the Step 0 crop (background
#                            noise — hands, soil, pots — never reaches a
#                            classifier) and its raw { label, score } is mapped
#                            into the SAME AnalysisData shape, so the text
#                            stage never learns which model produced the data.
#                            Pin a replacement with HF_VISION_MODEL.
#                          • Step 2 PRIMARY TEXT MODEL — concise Arabic reply
#                            from an open, non-gated, lightweight Qwen chain
#                            through the official Inference Providers router
#                            https://router.huggingface.co/v1/chat/completions
#                            (Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 → Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct
#                            → Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct). The router picks a live
#                            provider per model and the account's monthly free
#                            Inference Providers credits apply. Its ONLY job
#                            is to narrate the AnalysisData. A reply is
#                            REJECTED (and Step 3 takes over) when it is
#                            empty/nonsensical or does not correspond to the
#                            AnalysisData. If every text model is unavailable,
#                            a built-in formatter still answers from the
#                            analysis (source: "direct", never a 500).
#                        HF_TOKEN (Hugging Face's own variable name) is
#                        honoured as an alias when HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY is
#                        unset/blank. With neither set, Step 0/Step 1-fallback
#                        and Step 2 are skipped instantly (no request, no
#                        error, no delay) and Gemini answers when it is
#                        configured.
#
# Every response carries analysisSource ("gemini" | "mobilenet" | null) and
# textSource ("huggingface" | "gemini_fallback" | null) for logging and
# analytics; the user only ever sees the final reply.
#
# Set at least one of the two keys on the server at runtime (including on
# Vercel): each stage is optional and degrades to the next one. Only when BOTH
# keys are missing/blank does the route answer HTTP 503 with code MISSING_KEYS.
# Every other outcome answers HTTP 200: upstream AI failures degrade to
# built-in direct replies ({ source: "direct" }) — the API never returns 500.
# Never expose these secrets with a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# HF_LEAF_DETECT_MODELS (OPTIONAL, server-only) — Step 0 "Detection &
#                        Cropping" detector chain, tried in order. Step 0
#                        runs an open-source object detector BEFORE the image
#                        stage: the leaf is localised and the photo is cropped
#                        with sharp, and ONLY the cropped pixels are forwarded
#                        to the MobileNetV2 FALLBACK classifier. (Gemini, the
#                        primary image model, reasons over the full scene and
#                        always receives the untouched frame.)
#                        Default detector (open weights, free hf-inference
#                        tier): facebook/detr-resnet-50 (COCO DETR-ResNet-50,
#                        plant/leaf-labelled boxes only — the obsolete
#                        fine-tuned PlantDoc checkpoint is gone). Override
#                        with a comma-separated list of Hub ids, e.g.:
#                        HF_LEAF_DETECT_MODELS=myorg/leaf-detector
#                        When the detector is unavailable or finds no leaf,
#                        the untouched original frame is classified instead
#                        (the outcome is reported in the `preprocessing`
#                        field of the API response).
#
# HF_VISION_MODEL (OPTIONAL, server-only) — Step 1 FALLBACK image model, the
#                        PlantVillage classifier, a single Hub id that replaces the default
#                        linkanjarad/mobilenet_v2_1.0_224-plant-disease-
#                        identification (e.g. a self-hosted/retrained
#                        checkpoint). Unset = the clean MobileNetV2 default.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEYS=
GEMINI_API_KEY_2=
GEMINI_API_KEY_3=
GEMINI_MODEL=
HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY=
HF_LEAF_DETECT_MODELS=
HF_VISION_MODEL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REMOVED — CodeCraft vision engine (CODECRAFT_API_KEY / CODECRAFT_BASE_URL /
# CODECRAFT_VISION_MODEL). The orchestrator analyses the photo with Gemini
# first and only falls back to the MobileNetV2 PlantVillage classifier on
# Hugging Face; the CodeCraft gateway is never called (its WAF 403 stalls and
# the extra round-trip only added latency). Leftover CODECRAFT_* variables in
# an existing environment are ignored and can be deleted.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Real field data — Sentinel-2 NDVI over a farmer-drawn boundary.
#
# Server-side only: these never reach the browser. Without them the field map
# still draws, plots still save, and the heatmap still works — it simply says
# "no data available yet" instead of showing an estimate, because no
# measurement exists. See docs/field-data.md.
#
# Register at https://identity.dataspace.copernicus.eu/auth/realms/CDSE/account
# and create an OAuth client (a Sentinel Hub dashboard client, id "sh-…", is
# the right kind) to read the client id and secret. The free tier carries a
# monthly processing quota shared across the account.
#
# NDVI is read through the Sentinel Hub Process API on
# sh.dataspace.copernicus.eu with the SAME token and the SAME two variables.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CDSE_CLIENT_ID=
CDSE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The two URLs below are the defaults; you only need them to point at a mirror.
CDSE_TOKEN_URL=https://identity.dataspace.copernicus.eu/auth/realms/CDSE/protocol/openid-connect/token
CDSE_PROCESS_URL=https://sh.dataspace.copernicus.eu/api/v1/process
CDSE_CATALOG_URL=https://sh.dataspace.copernicus.eu/api/v1/catalog/1.0.0/search
CDSE_OPENEO_URL=https://openeo.dataspace.copernicus.eu/openeo/1.2
# LEGACY, OFF BY DEFAULT. Set to 1 to read NDVI through openEO instead of the
# Process API. An "sh-…" client is accepted by the token endpoint but rejected
# by openEO, which the app reports as "Copernicus rejected credentials".
CDSE_USE_OPENEO=
# LEGACY, OFF BY DEFAULT. The default satellite answer is the per-pixel NDVI
# raster (10 m, plot polygon) that the plot-details view paints; the dashboard
# heatmap's grid cells are derived from that same raster. Set to 1 to keep only
# the old rows/cols cell means and skip the per-pixel layer entirely.
CDSE_USE_GRID=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NASA POWER needs no key, no registration and has no documented hard rate
# limit, so there is deliberately nothing to configure here. Its base URL is a
# constant in src/lib/weather/power.ts rather than an env var: a mirror would
# silently change the data behind every number on the dashboard, and that
# should be a code change with a diff, not a deployment toggle.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
